Optimal Solution - Greedy single-pass solution to insert and merge an interval into a sorted list of intervals.
                   Process intervals in order: add non-overlapping ones, merge overlaps with the new interval, then append the rest.

class Solution {
    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] newInterval) {
        // Result array with maximum possible size
        int[][] res = new int[intervals.length + 1][2];
        // i -> pointer for input intervals
        // j -> pointer for result array
        int i = 0, j = 0;
        // Step 1: Add all intervals that end before newInterval starts
        while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]) {
            res[j++] = intervals[i++];
        }
        // Step 2: Merge all overlapping intervals with newInterval
        while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][0] <= newInterval[1]) {
            newInterval[0] = Math.min(newInterval[0], intervals[i][0]);
            newInterval[1] = Math.max(newInterval[1], intervals[i][1]);
            i++;
        }
        // Add the merged newInterval
        res[j++] = newInterval;
        // Step 3: Add all remaining intervals
        while (i < intervals.length) {
            res[j++] = intervals[i++];
        }
        // Return the result array trimmed to actual size
        return java.util.Arrays.copyOf(res, j);
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(N).
